## Network use

If you interact with this software, or a modified version of it, over a
network (for example through a hosted web or API service), section 13 of
the AGPL entitles you to receive the Corresponding Source of that version.
The operators of this service honor that entitlement for every user of the
network service, not only for those who received a copy of the software.

## Source offer

The Corresponding Source is the public source repository linked under
"Source" in the project README. Each release is a git tag built by the
release workflow (.github/workflows/release.yml), which regenerates the
component inventory (SBOM, docs/component-inventory.json) at the tag and
attaches it with sha256 checksums to the release, so the exact source
revision behind any released artifact is identifiable.

If the repository is unreachable or the deployed revision is not
identifiable, request the Corresponding Source via the contact channel
published under "Contact" in the README; the operators will provide it
under AGPL-3.0-or-later without further conditions.

## Corresponding source scope

"Corresponding Source" has the meaning given in section 1 of the AGPL: all
source code needed to generate, install, and (for an executable work) run
the object code and to modify the work, including scripts used to control
those activities. It does not include the operating system, system
libraries, general-purpose tools, or generally available free programs used
unmodified in performing those activities but not part of the work.

